add("dealer-add-on-cost-breakdown", "the finance-office add-on stack", "Separate physical and finance-office products from the vehicle price and test what is optional, marked up, duplicative, or poorly matched to the car.", "a protection package can be presented as a small monthly amount while adding thousands to financed debt", "buyer's order, F&I menu, service contract, GAP certificate, and accessory invoice", "remove or reprice add-ons before they become financed debt", ["paint protection", "VIN etching", "theft recovery", "wheel coverage", "key replacement", "service contract", "nitrogen tires", "accessory package", "appearance plan", "roadside benefit"], ["Classify the product before debating its price", "Separate a dealer-installed item from a contract benefit", "Trace markup through the amount financed", "Test whether coverage overlaps something you already have", "Read cancellation and refund mechanics", "Compare product value against the vehicle you actually drive", "Use the F&I menu as a negotiation record", "Document a clean decline without antagonizing the process"], "A buyer sees a $2,400 protection bundle quoted as $38 per month on a 72-month loan. The useful question is not whether $38 feels small; it is what the bundle covers, how much interest will accrue on it, and whether each component can be declined separately.", "Make the F&I menu earn its place in the deal", "Upload the buyer's order and add-on menu", "optional products and bundled markups", "The review flags language and math; you choose what fits your car and budget.");
add("hidden-auto-loan-fees", "the borrower's true cost of credit", "Find charges and conditions that increase the cost of borrowing beyond the advertised payment or nominal interest rate.", "a loan can look affordable while fees, term length, negative equity, or dealer-arranged rate compensation raise repayment cost", "retail installment contract, Truth in Lending disclosures, lender approval, and payoff statement", "compare credit offers by APR, finance charge, amount financed, and total of payments", ["APR", "finance charge", "amount financed", "total of payments", "late charge", "prepayment language", "dealer reserve", "negative equity", "loan term", "payment allocation"], ["Start with the four numbers that define borrowing cost", "Distinguish interest from other finance charges", "Follow a fee into the amount financed", "Measure the price of extending the term", "Check how a trade payoff changes the opening balance", "Compare dealer-arranged credit with a preapproval", "Read payment, late-charge, and payoff mechanics", "Build a side-by-side loan comparison before signing"], "Two offers show the same $540 payment. Offer A finances less at a shorter term; Offer B rolls negative equity and products into a longer loan. The lower-looking payment does not answer which borrower pays less. The contract numbers do.", "Know the dollars behind the monthly payment", "Upload the financing agreement and lender offer", "fees that inflate the effective borrowing cost", "Compare the same term and amount before accepting a lower payment.");
add("hidden-car-dealer-fees-guide", "the complete transaction taxonomy", "Map every line in a car deal into vehicle price, government charge, dealer charge, optional product, trade equity, or credit cost.", "a buyer receives a single total without knowing which amounts are pass-through charges, dealer revenue, optional products, or borrowed money", "advertisement, worksheet, buyer's order, trade appraisal, finance contract, and delivery receipt", "turn an opaque out-the-door quote into comparable line items", ["selling price", "dealer fee", "document fee", "electronic filing", "tax", "title", "registration", "trade payoff", "rebate", "optional product"], ["Build the fee taxonomy before negotiating", "Reconcile the advertised price with the written quote", "Separate official charges from dealer revenue", "Keep trade equity outside the vehicle price", "Audit tax, title, and registration assumptions", "Find optional products bundled into the total", "Compare two dealers on the same out-the-door basis", "Preserve a transaction record for later review"], "A quote advertises a $29,995 vehicle but the buyer's order reaches $34,800 before financing. A classification table can show whether the difference comes from tax, title, dealer fees, optional products, or a changed vehicle price.", "Turn the out-the-door total into visible lines", "Upload the complete quote or buyer's order", "unknown charges hidden inside the transaction total", "Start broad here, then use the specialist guides for a clause or product.");
add("auto-loan-fine-print-explained", "the clauses behind the payment", "Translate the clauses that control repayment, default, insurance, electronic payments, payoff, arbitration, and dispute rights.", "the payment is understandable but the rules governing missed payments, insurance, repossession, and disputes are not", "retail installment sales contract, payment authorization, insurance clause, and lender notices", "know what the loan permits, requires, and triggers after delivery", ["security interest", "payment due date", "grace period", "late charge", "force-placed insurance", "default", "acceleration", "arbitration", "electronic debit", "payoff quote"], ["Read the contract in the order a problem would unfold", "Understand the promise to pay and security interest", "Decode payment timing and allocation", "Study insurance and force-placed coverage language", "Identify default and acceleration triggers", "Handle arbitration and dispute provisions carefully", "Compare payoff, refund, and early-termination language", "Create a post-signing compliance calendar"], "A borrower believes a payment is on time because it was scheduled for the due date, but the contract defines when the lender must receive it and what happens after a returned debit. Fine print matters most when the account does not behave perfectly.", "Translate the clauses that govern life after delivery", "Upload the retail installment contract", "default, insurance, and payment-mechanics surprises", "The contract controls; use the review to prepare a precise lender question.");
add("vehicle-purchase-agreement-review-guide", "a line-by-line document audit", "Audit the buyer's order and related paperwork so the agreed vehicle, price, trade, products, and financing inputs match.", "the deal sounds right in conversation but the final documents contain a different VIN, trade payoff, product choice, or amount financed", "buyer's order, retail installment contract, trade documents, addenda, and odometer disclosure", "catch a mismatch before a signature turns it into a dispute", ["VIN", "stock number", "odometer", "buyer name", "seller name", "trade allowance", "payoff", "cash down", "rebate", "signature block"], ["Create a document order before reading the lines", "Verify identity and vehicle description", "Rebuild the purchase-price arithmetic", "Audit trade allowance, payoff, and equity", "Inspect products and acceptance boxes", "Reconcile the buyer's order to the finance contract", "Check blanks, handwriting, and addenda", "Make a final-copy packet before leaving"], "The buyer agreed to a $21,000 trade allowance, but the paperwork shows $19,500 and a separate payoff line. The review must distinguish allowance, payoff, and net equity; otherwise the difference can disappear into the new amount financed.", "Put every promise beside the line that proves it", "Upload the buyer's order and signed contract", "VIN, trade, and arithmetic mismatches", "Review every page before signing; fixing a line is easier than unwinding a deal.");
