add("dealer-markup-explained", "where dealership margin enters the transaction", "Explain vehicle-price adjustments, APR markup, lender buy rates, dealer reserve, product markup, and market adjustments as separate pricing decisions.", "the buyer cannot tell whether a higher number reflects the car, lender, finance product, or dealer compensation", "vehicle quote, lender approval, finance contract, product menu, and comparable preapproval", "identify the source of markup and negotiate the correct line", ["market adjustment", "lender buy rate", "contract rate", "dealer reserve", "APR markup", "product margin", "holdback", "stair-step incentive", "credit tier", "preapproval"], ["Name the type of markup before negotiating it", "Trace an APR from lender offer to contract", "Compare buy rate and contract rate carefully", "Separate vehicle market pricing from credit pricing", "Understand product margin without assuming fraud", "Use a preapproval as a controlled comparison", "Ask for a clean price and a clean credit offer", "Set a walk-away threshold with math"], "A lender approves 7.4% but the retail installment contract shows 9.4%. The difference is not explained by the vehicle price. Ask what rate was approved, what rate is contracted, and how dealer compensation enters.", "Find the source of the number that moved", "Upload the lender approval and finance contract", "vehicle, APR, and product markups", "A markup may be permitted and still worth negotiating.");
